===================================================================
                  GROUND.EXE VIRUS BEHAVIOR SUMMARY
===================================================================

1. DISGUISE AND REPLICATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The virus targets executable (.exe) and icon (.ico) files. It takes 
your original program, hides it deep in the system, and modifies 
its attributes to "System" and "Hidden". This makes the original 
file completely invisible in standard Windows Explorer.

2. REPLACEMENT WITH MALICIOUS COPIES
-------------------------------------------------------------------
In place of your original program, the virus creates a malicious 
duplicate with the exact same name. All of these virus copies have 
a fixed size strictly within the 521–523 KB range. When you try to 
launch your program, you accidentally execute the virus instead.

3. FILE RENAMING PATTERN
-------------------------------------------------------------------
To preserve the original files for its own technical reasons, the 
virus renames your real programs by adding a "g" character to the 
beginning of the filename (for example, "chrome.exe" is renamed to 
"gchrome.exe" and hidden).

4. PERSISTENCE IN SYSTEM MEMORY
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The virus injects itself into active system processes and creates 
registry keys for automatic startup. If you try to rename or unhide 
your files while the virus is still running in RAM, it will instantly 
detect the changes and lock or re-infect them all over again.
